---
title: "What is QQL?"
description: "Understand the qql-rs language, runtime, backends, and SDK family."
sidebar:
  order: 1
---

QQL is a query language and execution stack for Qdrant. It is implemented in
Rust and exposed consistently through native and WebAssembly bindings.

## The workspace

| Layer | Package | Responsibility |
|---|---|---|
| Language | `qql-core` | Lexer, strict parser, typed AST, validation, `inject_filter` |
| Planning | `qql-plan` | Transport-neutral `PlannedOperation` |
| Embedding | `qql-embed` | HTTP and host embedding interfaces |
| Runtime | `qql` | Preparation, batching, REST/gRPC execution |
| Edge | `qql-edge` | In-process vector storage and search |
| Hosts | `pyqql`, `@veristamp/nqql`, `qql-wasm` | Python, Node.js, and browser APIs |
| Tooling | `qql` CLI | Execute, explain, convert, dump, doctor, REPL |

## Where QQL fits

Use QQL when a system benefits from a reviewable query string, the same
retrieval behavior across languages, or AST-level policy enforcement.

Use the native Qdrant SDK directly when your application already owns a stable
typed request builder and does not need a portable query surface.

{% qqlExample title="Hybrid retrieval with a payload filter" %}
QUERY TEXT 'incident response' FROM runbooks
USING HYBRID DENSE dense SPARSE bm25 FUSION RRF
WHERE service = 'payments'
LIMIT 8;
{% /qqlExample %}

## Two boundaries to remember

{% aside type="caution" title="Isolation is not routing" %}
`WHERE tenant_id = 'acme'` or host-side `inject_filter` is the security
boundary. `SHARD 'acme'` only routes the request to a custom shard key.
Production multi-tenant systems commonly use both.
{% /aside %}

{% aside type="note" title="Parsing is not execution" %}
Parsing proves that the complete input is valid QQL. Planning can still require
collection schema, and execution can still require an embedder or a live
Qdrant backend.
{% /aside %}
